NiriFX development package for Arch Linux
========================================

This recipe builds main. Use the AUR helper's development-package update option
or rebuild to fetch new commits; ordinary version checks may not detect them.

Both packages contain the CLI, Studio, preset library, optional shell pickers,
complete NiriFX compositor and packaged login entry. Choose niri-fx for a
versioned release or niri-fx-git for main; they are alternatives with the same
features. No separate compositor package is needed.

Open NiriFX Studio from the application launcher, or run:

    /usr/bin/niri-fx studio --active

Install Chromium for an app-style window. Otherwise Studio opens the default
browser. Preview and JSON export work without a running compositor. Quickshell
and GTK pickers are optional. Choose a preset for each action, review the proposed
changes, then Apply. Stock Niri supports the documented open/close/resize effects;
the NiriFX session adds continuous drag, custom movement and swaps.

Prepare the NiriFX session
-------------------------

Installing this package does not activate its compositor or change user settings.
It installs a candidate at /usr/lib/niri-fx/session-candidate and a separate
"NiriFX (package)" login entry. Stock Niri remains installed for its session
service, shutdown handling, portal configuration and recovery entry. Its
executable, service and login files are not replaced.

As your normal user, review adoption with your Niri configuration:

    /usr/bin/niri-fx native adopt --config ~/.config/niri/config.kdl

Review the proposed files and plan_sha256. Repeat with --apply and
--expect-plan REVIEWED_SHA256 to copy the compositor, config and tools into
private retained storage and select them for the next login. Then log out and
choose "NiriFX (package)". An unprepared user receives an error; the login entry
never adopts a candidate automatically. Keep the ordinary Niri entry for recovery.

For later package versions, omit --config so the current settings are preserved:

    /usr/bin/niri-fx native adopt

Review the new plan before repeating it with --apply and --expect-plan.
Package updates alone do not change retained selections. Use /usr/bin/niri-fx
explicitly when adopting an update: an existing personal CLI launcher may still
point at the retained tools. Reopen system-package Studio to use updated tools;
refreshing an old page does not replace its running server.

Existing managed installations are preserved. Unsupported migration states are
refused with guidance instead of replacing user files. System Python minor
updates can require rebuilding the AUR package. Retained tools and compositor
copies still depend on the system Python and native libraries.

Desktop integration and recovery
--------------------------------

Install xdg-desktop-portal-gnome for screencasting and xdg-desktop-portal-gtk as the
fallback portal. GNOME's file chooser requires Nautilus unless the user explicitly
selects GTK for FileChooser. X11 applications need xwayland-satellite. Shell,
notification, secret-service and authentication-agent choices remain yours.

Use Studio Restore for effects applied to the stock configuration. Package
removal preserves user settings, profiles, Restore history and retained native
bundles; it removes the packaged candidate, dispatcher and chooser entry. Choose
stock Niri or reinstall the package to regain that chooser entry. Native session
rollback is a separate reviewed operation. Do not remove retained files or use
pacman --overwrite to resolve another user's login entry or launcher.

Build and acceptance scope
--------------------------

Both recipes use one NiriFX source tree for tools and native patches. The upstream
Niri revision is pinned. Locked Cargo dependencies are fetched during prepare();
native regression tests and the release/default-feature build run offline.
The producer strips its binary before recording its hash. makepkg's subsequent
strip/debug processing and C LTO are disabled; Rust's thin LTO stays enabled.

A package build and container adoption check do not establish display-manager
login, systemd session lifecycle, portals/PipeWire, physical input, lock/unlock,
suspend/resume, mixed monitors or GPU behavior. Those remain separate acceptance
gates. Building or installing a package never opens a session automatically.

Portable examples: /usr/share/doc/niri-fx/examples/
Current-source documentation: https://github.com/jturbide/niri-fx/tree/main/docs
Source and issues: https://github.com/jturbide/niri-fx
